WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.620
Speaker 1: So last week, we had a big discussion
across multiple days about should progressives

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.830
just now at this point work to kill the spending
and infrastructure bill that's been completely

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:11.830
gutted.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:17.620
I gave you a list last week of five six major
pieces of the proposals that had been floating

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:21.770
around that are believed to be completely
dead in the water.

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:27.240
And I said, at this point, I'm OK with progressive
saying, kill it.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.969
I often say if it does some good, then it
should be supported it because it's a step

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:34.010
in the right direction.

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:37.920
It will help some people in any opportunity
to help people should be taken.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.210
I started to go in the direction last week
of there's so little here that this will serve

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.710
as a victory for centrist Democrats by saying
we don't have to do anything progressives

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:46.710
want.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.589
It will serve as a victory for Republicans
for saying the more we obstruct, the less

00:00:49.589 --> 00:00:51.629
they're going to get, even if we're not in
control.

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:53.550
And it's nothing for progressives.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.559
We've got a voicemail related to killing the
bill, which I'm going to play for you in a

00:00:56.559 --> 00:00:57.829
little bit.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:01:01.850
One bit of news is that Bernie Sanders is
not giving up.

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:07.670
Senator Bernie Sanders appeared on CNN yesterday
and made it very clear that he's still pushing,

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.310
at least to get lower drug prices into the
bill.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:15.600
Now, if he was able to do that, is lower drug
prices, price controls on on medications and

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:16.600
prescriptions.

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.189
Would that be enough to to say, well, OK,
just for that alone, progressives should support

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:22.189
it.

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.189
We'll get to that in a second.

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:25.050
Let's first listen to some of this interview
he gave.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.930
Speaker 5: You have said there are major gaps
in this plan.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:34.400
Several of your priorities paid leave, dental
and vision for Medicare, giving the government

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.550
power to negotiate lower prescription drug
prices for seniors.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.320
That's not in the deal.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.649
You saw that House Progressive endorsed Doris
the compromise, do you?

00:01:44.649 --> 00:01:49.909
Speaker 6: Well, it's not in the bill yet,
but what I can tell you, Donna, we are working

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:50.909
right now.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:53.540
We spent all of yesterday on the telephone.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.659
We are paying the highest prices in the world
for prescription drugs.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:02.890
Are the pharmaceutical industry has spent
hundreds and hundreds of billions of dollars

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:06.899
to make certain that Americans pay ten times
more for some drugs than the Canadians or

00:02:06.899 --> 00:02:08.020
the Mexicans do.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:14.930
That fight continues, and the fight to expand
Medicare look poll after poll shows that the

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:20.129
American people understand it is not acceptable
that elderly people have teeth in their mouth

00:02:20.129 --> 00:02:21.129
that are rotting.

00:02:21.129 --> 00:02:22.129
They can't digest their food.

00:02:22.129 --> 00:02:27.230
They can't, they don't have the vision that
they need in order to read a newspaper.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.730
So we are continuing that effort.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:31.510
So can I just pains me very much?

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.510
Speaker 5: I'm sorry.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:33.510
Go ahead.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:35.480
No, I just want to just make sure that I understand
what you're saying.

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:37.410
You're saying you're continuing that effort.

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.980
You want to still add it before the House
votes.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.470
You want to add it to the bill that absolutely.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:47.050
Speaker 6: Look, we're working as everybody
should know on two separate bills.

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:52.120
You've got a bipartisan infrastructure bill
which will finally begin to address the reality

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:55.379
that our roads and our bridges and our water
systems are crumbling.

00:02:55.379 --> 00:02:59.270
That's one bill, but we want to move both
bills in tandem.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:03.739
And the second bill, the bill that deals with
the needs of the working class of this country

00:03:03.739 --> 00:03:08.349
in terms of child care, in terms of pre-K
that deals with the existential threat of

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:12.810
climate, that bill is still being worked on
literally today.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.030
It will be worked on tomorrow.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:18.150
I believe we're making some progress in making
an even stronger than it is.

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:23.769
Speaker 5: If it if you don't succeed in that
progress, will you support the framework as

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:25.079
it currently stated?

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:27.500
Question what the White House released at
the end of the week.

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:33.670
Speaker 6: Well, all I will tell you is we
have a very, very strong bill and it deals

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:41.370
with the fact that we are going to start paying
attention to the needs of working parents.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:42.370
Continue that $300.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.269
We're going to build affordable housing.

00:03:45.269 --> 00:03:49.360
We're going to make sure that elderly people
and people with disabilities can stay at home

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:50.360
rather than be forced.

00:03:50.360 --> 00:03:53.440
Speaker 1: So Bernie goes in to talk, you
know, these are these are talking points that

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:54.440
we've heard before.

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:59.200
But so the fundamental question ultimately
gets to, let's say, Bernie and other Democrats

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.020
get lower prescription drug price provisions
into the bill.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.189
Then should progressives support it?

00:04:06.189 --> 00:04:12.069
In my mind, that actually would be enough
to say support the bill, and it wouldn't change

00:04:12.069 --> 00:04:16.769
the fact that it would still be missing and
lacking so many things that were talked about

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:22.310
early attacks that target, you know, between
these two bills tax that targets the very,

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:29.610
very richest billionaires, paid leave, child
care, you know, so Medicare expansion, so

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:33.270
many things that I am in favor of and that
are good and that are necessary and that should

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.100
be in there.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:40.240
All of those things would still be gone, but
the degree to which the American people are

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.940
getting screwed when it comes to prescription
drug costs and it's so popular to talk about

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:51.900
lowering prescription drug costs, both Democrats
and Republicans talk about it all the time.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:52.900
Trump talked about it.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.060
He didn't do it.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.190
But the mechanisms through which they would
do it, of course, differ.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.780
There's no question about that.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:07.419
It actually would be so materially useful
to so many people, particularly seniors, the

00:05:07.419 --> 00:05:12.919
way that Bernie Sanders is talking about because
of how Medicare works.

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:15.090
That again, I'm talking pragmatically.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:21.420
I know the optics are terrible, but that is
so good for so many people that I would probably

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.520
say, don't kill the bill, get the bill done.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:28.090
If that's in there now, how likely that is
to happen and how much of this is Bernie either

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:31.460
posturing or trying to put public pressure
on colleagues.

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.930
And we just don't know.

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.950
We just these negotiations are happening constantly
between different parties, and this is why

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:42.030
my coverage of this has basically been listen,
the entire process is a grandstanding quagmire.

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:45.690
There's a lot of this that is just the worst
of politics and worst of the D.C. Beltway.

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.199
It doesn't relate to people who are just trying
to afford gas, trying to afford food, trying

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:50.220
to afford medication.

00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:51.970
It just doesn't connect.

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:59.699
It's so abstract and and idealized in some
way by those who think, Oh, big, negotiate.

00:05:59.699 --> 00:06:03.800
It's it's a lot of B.S. that's going on, and
I get all of that.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:08.450
But if there's really going to be something
actually done and if you trust Bernie, if

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:13.210
you're a Bernie supporter, if you believe
Bernie does the right things or tries to,

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:17.620
then you would imagine that if he gets that
into the bill, it's going to be materially

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:18.620
substantive.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:23.259
I think I'm probably back on and say, just
just pass it and get it done, and then let's

00:06:23.259 --> 00:06:26.060
move on and try to do more and do the next
iteration.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:32.220
If you disagree with me on the value of prescription
drug price controls as reason alone to support

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.419
the bill, I want to hear from you.

00:06:34.419 --> 00:06:36.880
Reasonable people can disagree, but it seems
like an important thing to me.

